$ORIGIN kyriakon.com.
$TTL 3600

; kyriakon.com - defensive registration.
; On-box path: /var/nsd/etc/kyriakon.com.zone.
;
; This domain never sends mail and serves nothing. It is registered so that
; nobody else can use the name, and every record below is a statement of "no".
; The wildcards matter: a forger can pick any name under a domain, not just the
; apex, so a per-name denial would leave the rest of the namespace open.
;
; The serial is separate from kyriakon.net's, since each zone refreshes on its
; own timer at Hurricane Electric.

@	IN	SOA	ns0.kyriakon.net. hostmaster.kyriakon.net. (
		2026091801	; serial - YYYYMMDDNN, bump on EVERY edit
		3600		; refresh
		900		; retry
		1209600		; expire - 14 days
		3600		; negative-cache TTL
		)

@	IN	NS	ns1.he.net.
@	IN	NS	ns2.he.net.
@	IN	NS	ns3.he.net.
@	IN	NS	ns4.he.net.
@	IN	NS	ns5.he.net.

; Null MX, RFC 7505: a record that says "this domain accepts no mail". Stronger
; than having no MX at all, because a sender with no MX falls back to the A
; record and will happily try to deliver there if one exists.
@	IN	MX	0 .
*	IN	MX	0 .

; SPF denial, apex and subdomains. -all with no mechanisms authorises nothing,
; and it is also what stops the domain being useful to someone forging it.
@	IN	TXT	"v=spf1 -all"
*	IN	TXT	"v=spf1 -all"

; Any selector, empty key: a forged signature claiming to come from this domain
; fails verification rather than merely being absent, which is the answer a
; receiver can act on.
*._domainkey	IN	TXT	"v=DKIM1; p="

; DMARC reject, with aggregate reports going to the alias already read on the
; .net side. Subdomains inherit p= per RFC 7489, so no sp= is required.
_dmarc	IN	TXT	"v=DMARC1; p=reject; rua=mailto:dmarc@kyriakon.net"

; Deliberately no A, AAAA or wildcard address record. Nothing is served under
; this name, so no hostname below it should resolve. A visitor typing the .com
; gets nothing rather than the wrong site, and the redirect to kyriakon.net is
; a separate decision that would need an address record and a certificate.
